2. World map geometry — games/geoklik/
--------------------------------------

The country outlines in the `DATA` array of games/geoklik/index.html are derived
from Natural Earth, 1:110m Admin 0 – Countries, re-projected into the game's
1000x520 coordinate box. Antarctica and the French Southern and Antarctic Lands
are omitted; the English country names come from the same dataset.

    Natural Earth — public domain.
    "All versions of Natural Earth raster + vector map data found on this
     website are in the public domain."
    https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/

    Natural Earth explicitly states that crediting the authors is unnecessary.
    The credit above is given voluntarily.


3. Flags — games/flag-quiz/
---------------------------

All 45 flags are generated by the `flagSVG()` function in
games/flag-quiz/index.html from colours, coordinates and trigonometry. Nothing
is imported. Five flags (South Korea, India, Israel, Morocco, Canada) were
previously embedded as pre-built SVG from the MIT-licensed lipis/flag-icons
project; those were removed on 26 August 2026 and replaced with the project's
own procedural renderers ('taegeuk', 'india', 'israel', 'morocco', 'mapleLeaf').

Some flags are deliberately simplified — Mexico's emblem is omitted, Brazil's
stars and banner are omitted, Argentina's Sol de Mayo is reduced to a disc with
straight rays. Flag designs themselves are national symbols and are not
licensed material.


4. Word lists — games/word-weave/, word-ladder/, type-rush/, anagram-blitz/,
   five-letters/, hidden-word/
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

All word lists are this project's own selection. They are filtered to lower-case
unaccented English headwords only: abbreviations, Roman numerals, accented
loanwords and proper nouns are excluded (see tools/filter-wordlists.py, which
produced the current lists and can be re-run). Individual words are facts and
carry no copyright; the selection and arrangement here are the project's own.


5. Public-domain games
----------------------

Many games in the collection implement traditional games whose rules are in the
public domain and which have no rights holder: chess, go, backgammon, draughts,
nine men's morris, mancala, dominoes, dots-and-boxes, Tower of Hanoi, peg
solitaire, gomoku, tangram, blackjack, and the Klondike, FreeCell, Spider and
Pyramid solitaire families. Every implementation in this repository is original
work; no third-party engine, opening book, level set or puzzle collection is
included.

Where a game is a re-implementation of a commercial title, it carries its own
name and its own artwork. See CLAUDE.md for the rule this project follows.
Famous single-word titles that are another company's mark were renamed:
Breakout -> Brick Buster, Asteroids -> Rock Drift, Pong -> Paddle Duel,
Flappy Bird -> Dot Glider, Tetris -> Block Cascade, Pac-Man -> Dot Muncher,
Frogger -> Road Hopper.


6. Names kept as generic terms - games/snake/, games/minesweeper/
-----------------------------------------------------------------

"Snake" is kept as the name of the mechanic, not as another company's title. The
mechanic dates to the 1976 arcade game Blockade and "snake" has since become the
generic descriptor for it, used by countless implementations; the collection
treats it the way it treats chess or sudoku above. Nothing of any particular
commercial version is reproduced - not its look, its palette, its levels or its
scoring. The implementation and artwork in games/snake/ are this project's own:
a tapering cyan-to-violet ribbon on a dark grid, drawn at runtime with Canvas 2D.

"Minesweeper" is treated the same way: the word describes the activity (clearing
a minefield by deduction) and is the generic name of the puzzle, implemented by
hundreds of programs since the 1980s. games/minesweeper/ reproduces none of any
particular version's presentation - the tiles, the number colours, the drawn
flags and mines and the timing are this project's own, drawn with CSS.

These are deliberate calls, not oversights. If either should be renamed the way
the titles above were, the change is mechanical: the game id and folder, the
i18n key prefix, the ASSETS entry and a CACHE bump.

7. Genre wording removed - games/whack-reflex/
----------------------------------------------

"Whac-A-Mole" is a registered mark of the arcade cabinet's maker, so the phrase
was removed from the description of games/whack-reflex/ in all twelve languages
on 9 September 2026; the texts now describe the game's own creatures instead.
The game's own name, "Whack Reflex", is kept: "whack" is an ordinary English
verb and the pairing is this project's, not a re-use of anyone's title. The
three pop-up figures are this project's own vector drawings (a glowing critter,
a crystal and a cracked core), drawn at runtime with Canvas 2D; no sprite,
sound or scoring table of any commercial version is reproduced.

"Sudoku" stays as it is, for the reason given in section 5: the puzzle's rules
are in the public domain and the word is its generic name. Every grid in
games/sudoku/ is generated at runtime by this project's own code, and the
generator keeps only grids with a single solution - no published puzzle set is
bundled.

8. Word list replaced - games/type-rush/
----------------------------------------

The falling-words game used to carry a 900-word English list whose provenance
was not recorded and which read like a sample of a spell-checker dictionary
(it contained obscure and vulgar entries). On 9 September 2026 it was replaced
with three lists written for this project - 125 short, 108 medium and 115 long
common English nouns, hand-picked and grouped by length. Individual words are
not protectable, but a compiled selection can be, so the collection now relies
only on its own list. The three tiers are also better play: every word is one a
learner recognises.

9. Tile faces drawn from scratch - games/tile-match/
---------------------------------------------------

The layout-clearing game is a tile-matching solitaire. Mahjong's tiles and the
matching solitaire built on them are traditional and have no rights holder, but
the game deliberately does not reproduce any published tile art: the faces are
this project's own - three colour groups numbered 1 to 9 with a drawn dot, bar
and diamond mark, plus seven abstract symbols - drawn at runtime with Canvas 2D
on an ivory tile with its own bevel. Every deal is generated by this project's
own code, which builds the board backwards from two free positions at a time so
that the deal can always be finished, and the hint follows that same line.

Klondike solitaire (games/solitaire/) and the rotating-pipe puzzle
(games/pipes/) are likewise traditional: the rules are public domain, the names
are generic descriptions, and the cards, card backs and pipe artwork are drawn
at runtime by this project's own code.

10. "Pexeso" kept as a generic term - games/pexeso/
---------------------------------------------------

The matching-pairs game keeps the name it is known by in Slovak and Czech.
"Pexeso" is the everyday word for the game in those languages, used by
countless publishers, and the collection treats it the way it treats snake and
minesweeper in section 6. The English name for the same game type is avoided
precisely because "Memory" is another company's mark. Nothing of any published
set is reproduced: the five symbol sets are system emoji chosen for this
project, and the cards, their lattice backs and the ivory faces are drawn with
CSS.

The same folder used to be a standalone app: it carried its own manifest, its
own service worker and its own copy of the twelve translations. Inside the
collection that second service worker would have kept serving its own cached
copies of the game after an update, so on 9 September 2026 the manifest and the
service worker were deleted, the registration was removed, and the translations
were moved into the shared i18n.js where every other game keeps them.

11. Folder rename and scoring - games/rock-drift/, games/dot-muncher/
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The rock-shooting game already carried its own title, Rock Drift, but its id,
its folder and its i18n prefix were still the marked arcade title. On
9 September 2026 the folder became games/rock-drift/, the id became
"rock-drift" and the prefix ast* became rdr*, so nothing in the collection
spells the mark any more. The word "asteroid" as the astronomical object still
appears in other games' texts; that is the plain noun, not a title.

games/dot-muncher/ kept its own maze, its own hero (a spinning cutter block)
and its own hunters (hexagonal drones), but its points were still the arcade
original's table - 10 a dot, 50 a pellet and 200/400/800/1600 for a chain of
hunters. Rule 5 in CLAUDE.md rules that out, so the values are now this
project's own: 12 a dot, 60 a pellet, 120/480/1080/1920 for a chain, and a
level bonus of 250 per cleared level.

games/rhythm/ bundles no audio at all. The beat grid is derived from the chosen
tempo by this project's own code, the note pattern is random within that grid,
and the hit sounds are short oscillator blips synthesised in the browser.

12. Hill Climb Racing distance - games/slope-rider/
--------------------------------------------------

The uphill driving game was called games/hill-racer/ with the prefix hill*,
and its title key held the half-Slovak string "Kopec Rally". Two things were
wrong with that. The name sat a word away from a well-known commercial title
of the same genre, and the title key existed in the English block only, so
every one of the twelve languages fell back to it. On 9 September 2026 the
folder became games/slope-rider/, the id "slope-rider", the prefix slr*, and
the title "Slope Rider" was written into all twelve blocks (transliterated for
Russian, Japanese, Korean and Chinese). The terrain, the car, the pickups and
the physics were and stay this project's own.

The rename list in section 5 now covers: Breakout -> Brick Buster, Asteroids ->
Rock Drift, Pong -> Paddle Duel, Flappy Bird -> Dot Glider, Tetris -> Block
Cascade, Pac-Man -> Dot Muncher, Frogger -> Road Hopper, Space Invaders ->
Alien Wave, Hill Climb Racing -> Slope Rider. Every one of those is renamed all
the way through - displayed title, game id, folder name and i18n prefix.

13. Description matched to the game - games/dead-grid/
-----------------------------------------------------

The zombie-wave game's description promised an "evacuation zone", and a green
ring was drawn in the middle of the arena, but standing in it did nothing. On
9 September 2026 the ring was given the function the text described - it heals
the player while they stand inside it - and the description in all twelve
languages was rewritten to say exactly that. Nothing in this game is taken
from anywhere: the arena, the walkers, the crates and the healing ring are all
drawn at runtime by this project's own code.

14. Scoring table replaced - games/sky-shield/
----------------------------------------------

The city-defence game awarded 25 points per intercepted missile multiplied by
the wave, and 100 points per surviving city - the arcade original's own table.
Rule 5 in CLAUDE.md rules that out, so on 9 September 2026 the values became
this project own: 18 points plus 6 per wave for an intercept, and 120 per city
still standing at the end of a wave. The game keeps its own name, its own
skyline, its own battery and its own explosion art, all drawn at runtime.

15. Scoring tables and colours replaced - games/bug-column/, games/blast-maze/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both games were rebuilt on 9 September 2026 and the two things that could be
read as taken from the arcade games of the same genre were replaced.

games/bug-column/ used to award 100 points for a crawler head and 300 for the
flanking spider - the same figures the 1980 arcade original used. The table is
now this project's own: 20 points per body segment, 75 for a head, 8 for a
destroyed pod, 180 plus 20 per wave for the spider, and 150 plus 50 per wave
for clearing a wave. The field's look was moved away from the original's red
and white mushrooms on black as well: the pods are drawn in the collection's
own teal-to-amber ramp (four hits, colour per remaining hit), the crawler is a
sky-blue chain with a violet head, and the flanker is pink. The creature
shapes, the ship, the band and every effect are drawn at runtime by this
project's own code.

games/blast-maze/ awarded 10 points per soft block and 200 per enemy, close
enough to the arcade bomb-maze table to be worth changing. The values are now
15 per crate, 250 per enemy, 60 per power-up and 400 plus 80 per level for
clearing the maze. The green field and brown bricks were replaced by the
collection's palette - indigo floor, steel pillars, amber crates, a pink
player orb and violet enemies. Neither game names, nor borrows art or code
from, any other product; "Bug Column" and "Blast Maze" are this project's own
titles, as are the folder names, the ids and the i18n prefixes.

The third game of the batch, games/orbit-escape/, needed no licensing change:
the name, the id, the prefix, the gravity wells, the debris, the fuel cells and
the exit portal are all this project's own, and its scoring (4 points per
second of flight, 200 plus 60 per level plus 2 per percent of fuel left at the
portal, 40 per fuel cell) was invented for it. The word on the portal is now
translated instead of the hardcoded English "EXIT".

16. Names, look and rules checked - games/moon-lander/, games/ninja-runner/,
    games/crown-jump/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

All three were rebuilt on 10 September 2026.

games/moon-lander/ was checked against the arcade mark first. The 1979 Atari
title is "Lunar Lander"; this game is called "Moon Lander", a plain
description of what the player does, and the folder name, the game id and the
i18n prefix (mln*) are this project's own. The rebuilt game also moves further
away from the arcade's visual expression: the wireframe-on-black look was
replaced by the collection's own - a gradient sky with a background planet, a
filled terrain body under a thin ridge line, a glowing landing pad with a soft
guide band, and the collection's glass HUD pills. Its scoring was invented
here: 120 points for a touchdown plus 2 per unit of fuel left plus 3 for every
pixel the pad is narrower than the difficulty's starting width. The physics
were rewritten in px/s and px/s2 so the game plays the same at any refresh
rate.

games/ninja-runner/ needed no licensing change - the name, the id, the ninja*
prefix, the runner, the shuriken, the hearts and the lantern night scene are
all this project's own, drawn at runtime. It was rebuilt for playability:
delta-time physics, a variable-height jump (hold the key or the button), three
difficulties, a best score per difficulty, a pause with a resume countdown,
and a device-pixel-ratio canvas. Three i18n keys that the old version used and
the new one does not (ninjaReset, ninjaNoLives, ninjaTryAgain) were removed
from all twelve language blocks.

games/crown-jump/ plays draughts, a traditional game in the public domain with
no owner; the name "Crown Jump", the id, the crj* prefix, the minimax opponent
and every drawn piece are this project's own. One rule detail was corrected
while rebuilding: move generation now takes the piece off its starting square
while it searches a capture chain, so a multi-jump that passes back through
its own starting square is found - the old code left the piece there and
silently blocked that move. Promotion in the middle of a jump still ends the
move, as English draughts requires, and capture stays mandatory.

17. Look, physics and records - games/tilt-maze/, games/cosmic-voyager/,
    games/echodrift/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026. None of the three needed a licensing change:
the names "Tilt Maze", "Cosmic Voyager" and "EchoDrift", the folder names, the
ids, the i18n prefixes (tlt*, voyager*, drift*), the mazes, the asteroid field,
the sonar levels and every drawn shape are this project's own, generated or
drawn at runtime. No third-party art, audio file, font file or code is used;
Cosmic Voyager's sounds are synthesised by the Web Audio API at runtime.

games/tilt-maze/ was rebuilt. Its description called it a tilt-controlled
marble maze, but the old version had no tilt at all - only a joystick. The
rebuilt game asks for the device-orientation permission when the player picks
"Tilt" on the card and then really rolls the marble by tilting, with the
joystick kept as the default and as the fallback when a device or browser
refuses. The board now scales to the window on a device-pixel-ratio canvas
(it used to be a fixed 320 x 320 canvas stretched by CSS), the friction is
converted to a per-second value so the roll is the same at any refresh rate,
the collision step is subdivided so a fast marble cannot pass through a wall,
and the best score is kept per difficulty instead of one shared number. Pause,
the resume countdown and the 12-language wiring follow the rest of the
collection. Six i18n keys that only duplicated shared ones (tltDifficulty,
tltEasy, tltMedium, tltHard, tltScore, tltBest) were removed from all twelve
language blocks.

games/cosmic-voyager/ kept its own engine and art. Its loop ran one update per
animation frame, so the game ran roughly twice as fast on a 120 Hz phone; it
now runs a fixed 60 Hz accumulator, so the logic ticks sixty times a second
whatever the display does. The canvas is device-pixel-ratio aware and its
backing store now matches its displayed size exactly (before, the buffer was
sized from the parent box while CSS displayed it narrower, which both blurred
the picture and skewed pointer aiming). A pause button, Escape/P and pausing on
lost focus were added, and the score, best score, start screen and game-over
screen were restyled into the collection's glass pills and gradient-bordered
card.

games/echodrift/ already had a device-pixel-ratio canvas, delta-time movement
and a pause, so it kept its engine and got the collection's look plus the one
thing it lacked: a record. It now stores the best star rating for each of its
three levels and shows both the level's record in the HUD and the total
collected on every overlay. Its overlay was also a fixed 430-pixel box; it is
now a full-screen backdrop with the card centred in it, which fixes a card
that sat off-centre on wide screens and one that slid to the left edge on
short ones.

18. Sharpness, fixed step and records - games/twin-drop/, games/gem-swap/,
    games/bubble-burst/, games/snip-rope/, games/wobble-tower/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026. None of the five needed a licensing change and
none was renamed: "Twin Drop", "Gem Swap", "Bubble Burst", "Snip Rope" and
"Wobble Tower" are this project's own titles, as are the folder names, the ids
and the i18n prefixes (twd*, gsw*, bub*, snp*, wob*). Every board, gem, bubble,
rope and block is drawn at runtime by this project's own code, and each game's
scoring was invented here, not taken from the commercial games of the same
genres - twelve points per cleared block plus fifty per chain step in Twin
Drop, base points plus fifty per chain in Gem Swap, twelve per popped bubble
and eighteen per dropped one in Bubble Burst, a hundred and fifty minus thirty
per extra attempt in Snip Rope, and twenty-five plus three per combo for a
perfect landing in Wobble Tower.

What was fixed in all five:

* Sharpness. Every one of them drew into a fixed-size canvas that CSS then
  stretched, so the picture was soft on any phone. The grid games (Twin Drop,
  Gem Swap) now size their cell in real device pixels, and the fixed-space
  games (Bubble Burst, Snip Rope, Wobble Tower) keep their logical coordinate
  space and scale it onto a device-pixel-ratio canvas. Pointer mapping was
  rewritten to match, and the displayed size is now exactly the drawn size, so
  nothing is stretched in one axis (checked at six window sizes).

* Frame rate. Bubble Burst and Wobble Tower advanced their physics once per
  animation frame, so they ran about twice as fast on a 120 Hz phone. Both now
  run a fixed 60 Hz accumulator. Twin Drop and Gem Swap were already
  time-based, and Snip Rope's physics is a fixed 1/60 step that had to stay
  untouched: its levels are pre-verified by a solver, and the cut window is
  stored per level, so any change to the step would invalidate them.

* Records. All five kept one shared best score across difficulties or board
  sizes; each now keeps a separate record per setting and reloads it when the
  setting changes.

* Pause. Snip Rope had none at all - it now has a pause button, Escape/P and a
  veil, and the four that had a pause now also pause when the window loses
  focus.

* Small screens. The shared old panel layout left the game area a strip on a
  phone in landscape - Bubble Burst was reduced to a fifty-pixel-tall field by
  a hardcoded "100vh - 350px" cap. The cap is now proportional, the panel turns
  into a row on short landscape screens so the board gets the full height, the
  data strip becomes compact, and the board no longer pushes the panel wider
  than the screen. Twin Drop's board grew from 132 to 276 pixels on a 375-wide
  phone as a result.

* Look. All five were moved to the collection's typography, glass cards,
  segmented controls and pill buttons.

19. Card and dice games - games/free-cells/, games/spider-cards/,
    games/pyramid-cards/, games/five-dice/, games/dice-gates/
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Reworked on 10 September 2026. All five play traditional games that belong to
nobody - open solitaire, spider solitaire, pyramid solitaire, a five-dice
category game and backgammon - and all five carry this project's own titles,
folder names, ids and i18n prefixes (fcl*, spd*, pyr*, fdc*, dgt*). Every card,
die, checker and board is drawn at runtime by this project's own code; no card
image, font file or third-party code is used.

One licensing change was needed. games/five-dice/ used the scoring table of the
best-known commercial version of the five-dice game: 25 for a full house, 30
and 40 for the two runs, 50 for five of a kind and a 35-point bonus at 63 in
the upper section. Those exact figures are that product's own table, and rule 5
in CLAUDE.md rules them out, so on 10 September 2026 they became this
project's: 30 for a full house, 25 for the small run, 45 for the large run, 60
for five of a kind, and a 40-point bonus at 60. The categories themselves (the
six faces, three and four of a kind, full house, the runs, five of a kind and
chance) are the old folk dice game's own and stay as they are. The translated
label already carried the target as a parameter, so all twelve languages picked
the new number up without a change.

What was fixed in all five:

* Sharpness and size. Each game drew into a fixed-size canvas that CSS
  stretched, so cards and pips were soft on every phone. All five now keep
  their logical coordinate space - so the tap mapping and the whole game logic
  are untouched - and scale it onto a device-pixel-ratio canvas. The size is
  taken from the panel rather than from the canvas's own wrapper (measuring the
  wrapper fed the old size back into the calculation), and it is recomputed
  once more after the first layout, because the first call runs before the
  panel is laid out. On a 375-pixel phone the board grew from 304 to 333
  pixels, and on a tablet from 304 to about 600.

* Records. games/dice-gates/ kept one shared best score for all three opponent
  strengths; it now keeps one per strength and reloads it when the strength
  changes. The other four already stored a record per difficulty.

* Look. All five moved to the collection's typography, glass cards, segmented
  controls and pill buttons, and the panel no longer sits under the back
  button on a narrow screen.

* Short screens. On a phone held sideways the panel keeps its column layout and
  the board takes its height from the window (the rest of the panel scrolls) -
  an earlier attempt to fold the panel into a row squeezed the board into a
  narrow strip, so it was dropped.
